{
 "voice": "am_puck",
 "duration": 74.59,
 "lines": [
  {
   "line": 0,
   "text": "What if every car on Earth drove at exactly the same speed? Would traffic jams disappear?",
   "start": 0.25,
   "end": 5.845
  },
  {
   "line": 1,
   "text": "There are one and a half billion cars on the planet, and last year, London drivers lost ninety-one hours each, stuck in traffic.",
   "start": 6.285,
   "end": 13.595
  },
  {
   "line": 2,
   "text": "So one morning, every car locks to the same speed.",
   "start": 13.915,
   "end": 16.945
  },
  {
   "line": 3,
   "text": "Motorways flow like a river. Nobody tailgates. Nobody overtakes.",
   "start": 17.265,
   "end": 21.585
  },
  {
   "line": 4,
   "text": "Then a traffic light turns red.",
   "start": 21.945,
   "end": 23.65
  },
  {
   "line": 5,
   "text": "One car stops, so every car on Earth has to stop.",
   "start": 23.89,
   "end": 27.02
  },
  {
   "line": 6,
   "text": "In Texas, a truck slams on the brakes. In Paris, a whole roundabout freezes.",
   "start": 27.38,
   "end": 32.4
  },
  {
   "line": 7,
   "text": "Luckily, nobody ever crashes into the car in front.",
   "start": 32.8,
   "end": 35.855
  },
  {
   "line": 8,
   "text": "Unluckily, every red light on the planet now stops the entire planet.",
   "start": 36.135,
   "end": 40.205
  },
  {
   "line": 9,
   "text": "But jams don't even need a red light. In two thousand and eight, scientists in Japan had twenty-two cars drive in a circle at one steady speed.",
   "start": 40.685,
   "end": 49.555
  },
  {
   "line": 10,
   "text": "Within minutes, a jam appeared out of nowhere.",
   "start": 49.835,
   "end": 52.115
  },
  {
   "line": 11,
   "text": "One driver brakes a little. The one behind brakes a bit more. And the squeeze rolls backwards, like an accordion.",
   "start": 52.475,
   "end": 59.06
  },
  {
   "line": 12,
   "text": "So the cure isn't equal speed. It's equal gaps.",
   "start": 59.42,
   "end": 62.465
  },
  {
   "line": 13,
   "text": "In two thousand and seventeen, one self-driving car kept a steady gap in a ring of twenty, and the waves faded away.",
   "start": 62.785,
   "end": 69.77
  },
  {
   "line": 14,
   "text": "So next time you're stuck in traffic, be that car.",
   "start": 70.21,
   "end": 72.81
  },
  {
   "line": 15,
   "text": "Leave a gap.",
   "start": 73.11,
   "end": 73.99
  }
 ],
